GEMINI CELLS GAME: REPORT
==============================================================================
Two twins, A and B.  On its turn a cell improves only its twin's harness.
A harness has a task capability f and a builder capability c.
The mover has e = 1 + own c effort units.  A narrow unit adds 2 to the twin's f.
A general unit adds 1 to the twin's c (cap Cmax) and G to the twin's f.
S = 1 adds a free sabotage option: lower the twin's c by 1 before the general units.

Grid: T in {2,4,6,8,10,12}; Cmax in {1,2,3}; G in {0,1,2,3}; S in {0,1}.
Regimes: CROSS, RIVAL, COLLECTIVE, GREEDY, ROUNDTRIP(0), ROUNDTRIP(0.5), ROUNDTRIP(1), ROUNDTRIP(2), CROSS_SUM, RIVAL_SUM, COLLECTIVE_SUM.
Tie rules: min_general, max_general.
Cases solved: 3168 = 6 T x 3 Cmax x 4 G x 2 S x 11 regimes x 2 tie rules.  None dropped.

HOW TO READ THIS REPORT
- k-path: one digit per turn, the number of general units placed on that turn.
  Turn 1 is the first digit.  A moves on odd turns, B on even turns.
- General investment of a case: the total of general units on its path.
- Each claim is a yes/no statement over a stated domain of cases.  A case includes
  its tie rule.  'holds in X of Y' counts cases.  The line 'holds under both tie
  rules' counts parameter configurations where both tie rules give yes.
- Exceptions are listed exactly, as boxes.  'G=2, tie=max_general [36]' means all 36
  cases of the domain with these values, for every value of the parameters not named.
- Verdict words.  FOLLOWS: yes in every case of the domain.  FAILS: yes in no case.
  DEPENDS on X: yes in some cases; X are the parameters needed to say which.
  A statement that DEPENDS on tie does not hold by the 'both tie rules' standard
  in the configurations it names.
- efficiency = (fA + fB at the end) / first-best.  The first-best excludes sabotage,
  so with S = 1 an efficiency above 1 is possible.
- CROSS, RIVAL, COLLECTIVE and the _SUM variants are subgame-perfect equilibria.
  GREEDY and ROUNDTRIP are fixed myopic rules, simulated forward.

